---
/**
 * website/src/components/Benefits.astro — the benefits (the owner's feedback of
 * 2026-10-19; README.md's `## Why Fugaro`).
 *
 * The brief: "we need a section with Benefits or somesuch as we have in the README, no?" So
 * the section is the README's own list of reasons, one line each — the four names the README
 * gives them, and for each one a sentence out of the paragraph that reason is argued in. This
 * page writes no benefit of its own: every name and every line is the README's, held to it
 * here at build time, which is what keeps a section whose whole job is to persuade from
 * becoming the one place on the site that says something the repository does not (Global
 * Constraint 6, D102: a page renders a fact and never restates one).
 *
 * **Four reasons, and no fifth.** `reasons()` below reads the README's `**N. Name.**`
 * paragraphs in the order the README writes them, and the build stops if the page does not
 * render exactly those: a reason the README drops, or one this page quietly leaves out, is a
 * section whose list is neither the README's nor shorter than it — and taking only the
 * claims the code and the docs support is a choice about *this* list, which is why the four
 * below are the four the repository backs, each named here with the document that carries it:
 *
 *   1. *Safe by construction* — the run is a throwaway container in the reader's own cloud
 *      project, so it does not read their laptop (`README.md## How it works`,
 *      `docs/design/v1.md`).
 *   2. *Scale out, and walk away* — one job execution per task, up to `max_parallel`
 *      (`README.md`, the same section, and `docs/design/v1.md`).
 *   3. *Team control in one place* — the project layer publishes the shared policy every
 *      repository inherits (`docs/project-layer.md`).
 *   4. *The newest tools and models, ready to use* — one pinned base image, and the model
 *      chosen per role (`docs/base-image.md`, `docs/multi-model.md`).
 *
 * **Why a line cites its own reason and not the section.** The four paragraphs are one
 * section, so a line held to the section alone could be moved from the reason above it to
 * the one below and still be a quotation, while the page then sold the reader the wrong
 * sentence under the wrong name. Each line is therefore read against the paragraph its own
 * reason is written in, and `website/test/home.mjs` makes the same check against the built
 * page from outside the build.
 *
 * **The names are the README's, with the number off.** `**1. Safe by construction.**` is a
 * list in the README and a heading here, and the number goes because the grid's own order is
 * the order: a number in front of a name is the README's device for counting reasons, and a
 * reader of the page counts four boxes. The names are marked up as `h3` under the section's
 * `h2`, so a screen reader gets four headings under one, in the README's order.
 *
 * **The owner's second feedback of 2026-10-19: "make them bigger, bolder, use an icon for
 * each point".** Each benefit therefore carries one glyph — one inline `<svg>` per `<li>`,
 * drawn with `currentColor` strokes in `--accent`, its box a step of the spacing scale — and
 * the type is a step up the type scale at every level of the block, so the four read as the
 * section's four points rather than as a paragraph broken into four. **The glyph is
 * decoration and says so**: `aria-hidden="true"` and `focusable="false"`, because the name
 * beside it carries the meaning and a screen reader given the glyph as well would read the
 * same point twice. It is inline SVG in this file and nothing else: no icon font, no image
 * file, no third-party anything, no JavaScript — a page of this site has to be readable with
 * the script off, and a glyph fetched from somewhere else is a request to somewhere else
 * (Global Constraints 4 and 8). `website/test/home.mjs` holds every benefit to exactly one
 * such icon from outside the build.
 *
 * **Both size steps, and why the section's own heading moved with them.** The type scale has
 * one step between the body size (`--font-size-base`) and the size above it (`--font-size-lg`),
 * and the feedback asks for the name *and* its one line to be larger, so both land on that
 * step: they are told apart by the weight and the colour the block already used, not by a size
 * this file would have to invent (there is no weight above `--weight-semibold` either, which is
 * the weight the name already carried — "bolder" here is that weight stepped up the scale).
 * The section's own `h2` goes one step further (`--font-size-xl`), so the block still reads as
 * a section with four points under it rather than as four headings and a label.
 *
 * **The heading is "Why Fugaro?"** (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-20, capital W and F and the
 * question mark). It was "Benefits", which named the block's shape rather than what it says:
 * the four points below are the README's `## Why Fugaro` reasons, and they are the answer to
 * that question, so the heading is the question and the list under it the answer. The section
 * carries `id="benefits-title"` and `class="home-benefits"` still — the id and the class are
 * the element's name in the markup, which `aria-labelledby` and the stylesheet both read, and
 * neither is a label a reader sees. `website/test/home.mjs` holds the heading to the exact
 * string, question mark included, and `website/test/slider.mjs` holds the panel's own heading
 * to the tab label that opens it, so the tab and the heading are one name and a rename that
 * reached only one of them stops the build.
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { REPO_ROOT } from '../content.ts'

/**
 * A string with its markdown markers and its whitespace removed: how a line and the
 * paragraph it claims to come from are compared, the same reading the other components use.
 * Emphasis and code spans are markers rather than text, and where a document breaks a line
 * is its layout rather than its words.
 */
function comparable(document: string): string {
  return document.replace(/[`*]/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, '')
}

/**
 * One `## <heading>` section of README.md, up to the next `## ` — the same reader
 * `website/test/home.mjs` uses, so the build and the check resolve a section the same way.
 * A heading the README does not carry is a defect in this file rather than in the README:
 * the section cites it by name, and a renamed heading has to fail here, naming itself.
 */
function readmeSection(heading: string): string {
  const readme = readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'README.md'), 'utf8')
  const start = readme.indexOf(`\n## ${heading}\n`)
  if (start === -1) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: README.md carries no "## ${heading}" section, and the benefits are the reasons that section argues (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19). Either the section was renamed or the page moved off it.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = readme.slice(start + 1)
  const next = rest.indexOf('\n## ', 1)
  return next === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, next)
}

/** The document the section is quoted from, as every message about it names it. */
const SOURCE = 'README.md#Why Fugaro'

/**
 * The reasons a section argues, in the order it writes them: each `**N. Name.**` paragraph,
 * as its name and the paragraph whole — the paragraph because a line is held to its own
 * reason and not to the section. A `## Why Fugaro` that stopped writing its reasons as
 * numbered bold paragraphs is a defect in this file's reader rather than in the README, and
 * it fails here with the section's name on the message.
 */
function reasons(section: string): { name: string; paragraph: string }[] {
  const out: { name: string; paragraph: string }[] = []
  for (const block of section.split(/\n{2,}/)) {
    const match = /^\*\*\d+\.\s*(.+?)\.\*\*/.exec(block.trim())
    if (match) out.push({ name: match[1], paragraph: block })
  }
  if (out.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: ${SOURCE} carries no "**N. Name.**" reason paragraph, and the benefits are those paragraphs read in order (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19). Either the section was rewritten or this reader is out of date.`,
    )
  }
  return out
}

const REASONS = reasons(readmeSection('Why Fugaro'))

/**
 * The four reasons the page renders, in the README's order: the name the README gives each
 * one, and one line of the paragraph it is argued in. Nothing here is written for the page —
 * `line` is a sentence of the README's own reason paragraph, and the loop below is what
 * makes that true of the artifact rather than of this file.
 *
 * `icon` names the glyph the benefit is drawn with, and it is a key into `GLYPHS` rather than
 * markup, so the guard below can hold the four to one distinct glyph each: the glyph is
 * chosen for the point it sits under — a shield for a run that is safe by construction,
 * arrows leaving a centre for a fan-out, three people for a team's shared policy, a spark for
 * the newest tools — and the four are not illustrations of one another.
 */
const BENEFITS = [
  { name: 'Safe by construction', line: 'The agent does not run on your machine.', icon: 'shield' },
  { name: 'Scale out, and walk away', line: 'Each task is its own job execution', icon: 'arrows' },
  { name: 'Team control in one place', line: 'Policy lives in the project, not on each laptop.', icon: 'team' },
  { name: 'The newest tools and models, ready to use', line: 'You choose the model per role', icon: 'spark' },
]

/**
 * The glyphs a benefit may be drawn with: four, and one point each (above). Each entry **is**
 * the drawing — the shapes the `<svg>` in the markup below holds — rather than a name the
 * template is expected to have a branch for, so a name and the picture that goes with it
 * cannot come apart: an entry with no shape in it is refused by the guard at the foot of this
 * block, and the section's `<svg>` renders whatever the entry holds. A rename that leaves the
 * drawing behind is therefore a build that stops here, not a point rendered with an empty
 * `<svg>` the way it would be if the drawing lived in the template's own branches.
 *
 * Inline SVG, one box of 24 by 24 user units, drawn with `currentColor` strokes by the rule
 * that carries `.benefit-icon`. No `<use>`, no `<image>`, nothing fetched: a glyph a reader's
 * browser has to download is another request, and this page's own drawing is shapes in its
 * markup (Global Constraints 4 and 8).
 */
const GLYPHS = {
  /* A shield: the run is a throwaway container in the reader's own project. */
  shield: '<path d="M12 3l7 3v6c0 4-3.5 6.5-7 8-3.5-1.5-7-4-7-8V6Z" />',
  /* Two arrows leaving a centre: one task, many job executions. */
  arrows: '<path d="M11 13L19 5M13 5h6v6M13 11L5 19M11 19H5v-6" />',
  /* Three people: the policy the project layer publishes, and everyone under it. */
  team:
    '<circle cx="12" cy="7.5" r="3" />' +
    '<circle cx="5.5" cy="10.5" r="2.5" />' +
    '<circle cx="18.5" cy="10.5" r="2.5" />' +
    '<path d="M6.5 20.5c0-3 2.5-4.5 5.5-4.5s5.5 1.5 5.5 4.5" />' +
    '<path d="M2 20.5c0-2.4 1.5-3.8 3.2-4.1" />' +
    '<path d="M22 20.5c0-2.4-1.5-3.8-3.2-4.1" />',
  /* A spark: the newest tools and models, ready to use. */
  spark: '<path d="M12 3l2.2 5.8L20 11l-5.8 2.2L12 19l-2.2-5.8L4 11Z" />',
}

/** The shapes a glyph is drawn with: what has to be in an entry for it to draw anything. */
const GLYPH_SHAPES = /<(?:path|circle|ellipse|rect|line|polyline|polygon)\b/

// Hold every benefit to the reason it is, in the reason's own paragraph, here, so a
// paraphrase — or a line that moved from the reason above it to the one below — stops the
// build naming the file rather than reaching a reader as the repository's own words.
{
  if (BENEFITS.length !== REASONS.length) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: the page renders ${BENEFITS.length} benefit(s) and ${SOURCE} argues ${REASONS.length}: the section is the README's reasons, and one dropped is a claim the README makes that the page does not (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).`,
    )
  }
  for (const [index, benefit] of BENEFITS.entries()) {
    const reason = REASONS[index]
    const position = index + 1
    if (reason.name !== benefit.name) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: benefit ${position} is called ${JSON.stringify(benefit.name)} and ${SOURCE}'s reason ${position} is called ${JSON.stringify(reason.name)}: the page renders the README's reasons in the README's order, with the numbers off (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).`,
      )
    }
    if (!comparable(reason.paragraph).includes(comparable(benefit.line))) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: benefit ${position}'s line ${JSON.stringify(benefit.line)} is not in ${SOURCE}'s reason ${position}, and every line of the benefits is a sentence of the reason its name names rather than one written for the page (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19; Global Constraint 6). Either the reason was reworded or the line was edited.`,
      )
    }
    if (GLYPHS[benefit.icon] === undefined) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: benefit ${position}, ${JSON.stringify(benefit.name)}, is drawn with ${JSON.stringify(benefit.icon)}, which is no glyph this section draws (${Object.keys(GLYPHS).join(', ')}): the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19 asks each point for an icon, and a glyph a benefit names and the section has no drawing for is a point rendered with an empty <svg>.`,
      )
    }
  }
  // No glyph in the map is empty. This is the half the membership check above cannot
  // give: an entry that is an empty string (or a comment, or a group with nothing in it) is a
  // name the section "has" and a `<svg>` with no shape in it — an invisible icon, which is the
  // defect the owner's feedback is about, arriving with the build green.
  for (const [name, shapes] of Object.entries(GLYPHS)) {
    if (!GLYPH_SHAPES.test(shapes)) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: the glyph ${JSON.stringify(name)} holds no shape (${JSON.stringify(shapes)}), and a glyph with no shape in it is an empty <svg>: every point of the benefits is drawn with a picture (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19), and an entry with nothing to draw is a name with no icon behind it.`,
      )
    }
  }
  // One distinct glyph per point (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19): four benefits drawn
  // with the same picture are one icon used four times, and the picture then says nothing
  // about the point under it.
  const drawn = new Set(BENEFITS.map((benefit) => benefit.icon))
  if (drawn.size !== BENEFITS.length) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/Benefits.astro: the ${BENEFITS.length} benefits are drawn with ${drawn.size} distinct glyph(s) — ${[...drawn].join(', ')}: each point carries a glyph of its own (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).`,
    )
  }
}

/**
 * **Where the section's gutter stands down.** A page that renders this section as a block of
 * its own gets the gutter and the room below with it (the rule below, and the round-6 fix that
 * put it here). The homepage renders it inside a panel of `HomeSlider.astro`, where the panel
 * is the block: the two gutters would stack, and the section's text would sit 32 px inside the
 * other panels'. So the caller says so, and the section's own rule is what stands down. It is
 * decided here rather than reached into from the panel with a `:global` selector, for the
 * reason the round-6 fix is about: a block's rule belongs where the block is rendered.
 */
const { inPanel = false } = Astro.props
---

<section
  class:list={['home-benefits', inPanel && 'home-benefits-panel']}
  aria-labelledby="benefits-title"
  data-source={SOURCE}
>
  <h2 class="benefits-title" id="benefits-title">Why Fugaro?</h2>
  <ul class="benefit-list">
    {
      BENEFITS.map((benefit) => (
        <li class="benefit">
          {/* The point's glyph: inline, decorative, and drawn with the accent by the rule
              below (`stroke: currentColor` reads the `color` that rule sets). **The drawing is
              the map's, not a branch here** (`GLYPHS`, above): a template branch per name could
              be out of step with the map by one word and render an empty `<svg>` with nothing
              to see it, and a name whose entry holds no shape is refused at the foot of the
              frontmatter. The shapes are markup and nothing else — no `style` attribute, which
              the deployed policy's `style-src` would have to admit, and no request: a
              `<use href>` or an `<image>` is a file this page would fetch, and the page
              fetches nothing for its own drawing (Global Constraints 4 and 8). */}
          <svg
            class="benefit-icon"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
            focusable="false"
          >
            <Fragment set:html={GLYPHS[benefit.icon]} />
          </svg>
          <h3 class="benefit-name">{benefit.name}</h3>
          <p class="benefit-line">{benefit.line}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): the section names the palette, the spacing scale, the type scale and
     the radii, and defines none of them.

     **The section's own gutter and its room below are declared here, in this component's rule**
     (review round 6). They were in `src/pages/index.astro`'s `.home-chat, .home-flow,
     .home-benefits` rule, and Astro scopes a page's selector to the elements the page renders:
     the `<div class="home-chat">` and `<div class="home-flow">` carry the page's scope, and this
     `<section>` is rendered by this component and carries `Benefits.astro`'s — so the page's
     rule never matched the section, the section kept no padding at all, and its text ran edge to
     edge on a phone. The rule is where the element is rendered, the same as `Hero.astro`'s and
     `HomeInstall.astro`'s; and it is one block's rule rather than the page's three, so a block
     that pads both sides of a gap is now visible in one file. The gap is padded once: this
     section pads below itself and not above, which is what
     `website/test/base-styles.mjs`'s `homeRhythmFailures` refuses a pair of blocks for. */
  .home-benefits {
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  /* Inside the homepage's slider the panel is the block (see `inPanel` above): the section
     keeps its heading, its list and its glyphs, and gives up the column and the room that the
     panel around it already carries. */
  .home-benefits-panel {
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* The section's own label, one step above the point names under it and above the size the
     page's other section headings are set in: the feedback of 2026-10-19 moves the four points
     up one step of the type scale, and the block as a whole up two — one for the points, one
     for this heading — because a section whose label stayed where it was would read as the
     smallest thing in its own block. Nothing else about the heading moves. */
  .benefits-title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The grid, and its one departure from the site's measure. `base.css` holds lists to the
     measure (`:where(p, ul, ol, dl, blockquote, figcaption) { max-width: var(--measure) }`),
     and two columns of a 68 ch box is a column of two words. The list is not prose; it is
     the section's grid, and it is as wide as the page's column — the same escape, and the
     same reason, as the flow's own list. */
  .benefit-list {
    max-width: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-lg) var(--space-xl);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Two columns from 40 rem up: four reasons read as a two-by-two rather than as a column
     of four, and the column repeats rather than `auto-fit`, so the grid is the same shape at
     every width above the breakpoint instead of re-flowing into a row of three and a
     widow. */
  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .benefit-list {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* The benefit: a glyph, a name and its one line, with the site's own separator above them
     rather than a card — a rule is what the site already draws between things that are not one
     thing, and the icon the feedback of 2026-10-19 asks for is drawn inside the item. */
  .benefit {
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
    border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
  }

  /* The glyph: one box per point, a step of the spacing scale (48 px), drawn a line above the
     name it belongs to. **The box is the glyph's box, and it is `display: block`** so a phone
     reader sees the icon on its own line rather than as an inline mark set into the name — and
     so the word-seam gate reads the boundary a reader sees (`website/test/spacing.mjs`). The
     accent is the stroke: `--accent` on `--ground` is a pair `a11y.mjs` computes at 3:1 in all
     four palettes, which is the ratio 1.4.11 asks of a graphic rather than prose. */
  .benefit-icon {
    display: block;
    width: var(--space-xl);
    height: var(--space-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .benefit-name {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xs);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    /* Larger and at the top of the weight scale: the feedback asks for "bigger, bolder", the
       type scale has one step between the body size and this one, and `--weight-semibold` is
       the weight the site's scale stops at. */
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--text);
    /* A name longer than its column breaks rather than running out of it. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The line, muted and a step larger than the body size it was set in: `--text-muted` on
     `--ground` is a pair `a11y.mjs` computes at 4.5:1 in all four palettes, the same
     requirement as body text rather than a decorative grey, and the name above it is told
     apart by weight and colour rather than by a size the scale does not have. */
  .benefit-line {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
